        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tananachiefs.org/about/communities/tanana/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1898 Fort Gibbon was founded at Tanana to keep the telegraph line running between Fairbanks and Nome.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tananachiefs.org/about/communities/tanana/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The telegraph post sparked a boom, bringing steamboats and gold seekers, and turning Tanana into a bustling river port.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tanana%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During World War II Tanana’s airfield served as a stop for aircraft heading to Russia under the Lend‑Lease program.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tanana%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A White Alice communications site was built nine miles behind Tanana in the 1950s as part of the Cold War Distant Early Warning system.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tanana%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In the 1930s a regional hospital opened, serving northern Alaska until 1982, and the Native Village of Tanana was officially chartered that decade.",
